    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule: **reject creating an owner whose lower-cased email is already used by any other owner, responding with 409 Conflict.**\n\nFollowing the codebase's existing conventions for duplicate-detection rules (mirroring `DuplicateOwnerTelephoneException`), I made three changes:\n\n1. **New exception** \u2014 `DuplicateOwnerEmailException` (`rest/advice/`), a `RuntimeException` carrying the offending email.\n\n2. **Controller wiring** (`OwnerRestControllerV1`) \u2014 added a `rejectDuplicateEmail(...)` call in `addOwner` right after the telephone check, plus the `rejectDuplicateEmail` method. It runs against the already-normalized (trimmed + lower-cased) email, lower-cases each existing owner's email for comparison, ignores owners with no email (email is optional), and skips entirely when the new owner has no email.\n\n3. **Exception handler** (`ExceptionControllerAdvice`) \u2014 a `@ExceptionHandler` mapping `DuplicateOwnerEmailException` to `HttpStatus.CONFLICT` (409) with an `errors: [\"email\"]` property, matching the shape of the sibling 409 handlers.\n\nTest result: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`. The acceptance test `coreRejectsDuplicateEmail` (creates an owner with an email, then attempts a second owner reusing that email and expects 409) passes.",
